  Cheap to change later: the generation logic. Expensive: the semantics — every future form and every log line already
  written assumes one meaning, and switching means your historical data no longer lines up.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m the stable per-browser id. It matches the intent behind "track who logged in" across sessions,
  keeps PII out of storage, and a per-login id can be layered on top later as a second field without invalidating
  anything.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What should the userId identify, and how long should it live? → Stable per-browser id (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last question before I lay out approaches.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether this needs a transport, or just a value. Right now [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a stub that [38;5;153mconsole.log[39ms and returns a
  fake result — nothing leaves the browser.

  - [1mLog fields only[0m — the id gets attached to existing console output and is available to future forms. No network code,
    nothing to mock in tests. This is the smallest thing that makes the id real.
  - [1mAttach to outgoing requests[0m — the id rides along on the eventual [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m call as a header or body field. Means
    designing where it attaches now, but the stub has no real request yet, so there's a placeholder either way.
  - [1mDedicated analytics sink[0m — a separate [38;5;153mtrack()[39m path that emits events somewhere. That's a second subsystem, and I'd
    want to push back on building it before you know what events you want.

  Cheap to add later: the transport, if the module owning the id has a clean interface. So I'd rather get the boundary
  right and leave the wire for when there's a real endpoint.
